course: dictionary
lesson: 7340
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the context tells you which one is meant. The word plaster is a good example. «Tell a grown-up about a blister early and cover it with a plaster» means a small sticky strip for your skin, because you cover a blister with it.
explain: The same word can mean something very different. «Palaeontologists wrap big bones in plaster jackets» means a hard paste that dries to protect the bone. Look at the words around it: wrap, jackets and carry safely are the clues.
explain: Plaster can also be a verb, an action word. In «plastered with mud», the mud covers something thickly. Ask yourself: is it a thing or an action? If it is an action, the meaning will be about doing something to something else.
fact: A word can have several meanings, so always check the context before you decide what it means.
fact: «Plaster» (noun) means a small sticky strip put over a cut, called a Band-Aid in the USA and Canada.
fact: «Plaster» (noun) also means a paste that goes hard when dry, used to make walls and ceilings smooth.
fact: A «plaster cast» is a hard cover put around a broken arm or leg while the bone heals.
fact: «Plastered with» (verb) means covered thickly, as in «plastered with mud».
exercise: «Tell a grown-up about a blister and cover it with a plaster.» What is a plaster here? | *A small sticky strip for your skin | Mud spread on a path | A hard cover for a broken leg | A paste for smoothing walls
exercise: «The builders plastered the kitchen walls.» What does plastered mean here? | Washed the walls with clean water | Hit the walls with a ball | Tore the walls down into pieces | *Covered the walls with a smooth paste
exercise: Why does the doctor put a plaster cast on Lily's broken arm? | To make the arm look pretty | *To keep the bone still while it heals | To wash the arm clean | To stop the arm from growing
test: «Mud plastered his trousers after the football match.» What does plastered mean here? | *Covered thickly | Torn into pieces | Dried in the sun | Washed clean
test: Which meaning fits «wrap big bones in plaster jackets»? | A kind of cake | *A hard paste put around something to protect it | A cover to keep the bone warm | A small sticky strip for skin
exam: Sam says «plaster» always means a sticky strip for cuts. What is the best answer? | No, it only means a hard cast | Yes, it always means a sticky strip for cuts | No, it means a kind of bread | *No, it can also mean a paste for walls, a hard cast, or to cover thickly
